WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:12.370
Malcolm Grant: May we then move on to the
business plan for 2015/16? I am going to invite

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.089
Karen to introduce that for us, please.

00:00:15.089 --> 00:00:22.089
Karen Wheeler: Thank you, Malcolm. The business
plan this year reflects a lot of work that

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:27.199
we have been doing. Clearly the business plan
is an important document. It sets out the

00:00:27.199 --> 00:00:32.730
work that we really need to do this year to
take forward, not only the delivery of our

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:39.710
mandate, but also the critical change work
that we need to start initiating and that

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:46.710
is presaged in the Five Year Forward View,
to ensure that the mandate and outcomes can

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:54.700
be delivered in years to come on a financially
sustainable basis.

00:00:55.309 --> 00:01:02.309
The business plan this year, we have tried
to respond to lots of comments that we have

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:09.420
received through the year to focus down onto
ten key priorities, rather than trying to

00:01:12.849 --> 00:01:19.380
cover everything that we do. Focusing on ten
key priorities where we need to make a significant

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:25.330
step-change and difference during the course
of this year.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:32.330
Those priorities cover a number of themes
that will endure even though our priorities

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:38.060
this year may change from the priorities that
we choose to take forward next year. The themes

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:45.060
that we are working within are likely to endure.
Those are in transforming care for patients

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:51.849
and particularly this year we are focusing
on making the step-change in cancer, in mental

00:01:51.849 --> 00:01:58.849
health, learning disabilities and preventing
diabetes. In designing services around patients

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:09.000
and transforming those services. We also have
a big priority associated with achieving financial

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:17.280
sustainability through making whole system
change, obviously working very closely with

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:23.110
all of our partners in the system. Underpinning
all of that, we need to put some significant

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:30.110
enablers in place to drive and support all
the change across the system.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:38.980
We had a discussion with Board members yesterday
and we are in the process of capturing and

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:47.020
finalising the Business Plan and we would
like to ask the Board today to delegate approval

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:55.780
to Malcolm and to Simon for finalising that
so it can be published tomorrow. The other

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:02.410
thing I should say is we have been doing a
lot of work as well around our budget allocations

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:08.190
to ensure that our budgets are aligned with
and support all of our core priorities.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:15.190
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Karen.
Let me invite comments from members of the

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:17.660
Board. Ciaran?

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:22.710
Ciaran Devane: Karen, thank you for the swan-like
delivery of that. I think we all know there

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:29.710
was a lot more paddling underneath. Significant
enablers, can I just pick you up on the word?

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:34.300
If we are going to make these changes, one
of the ten priorities is this whole system

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:38.990
change, which is slightly a large priority,
we are going to have to do things in a very

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:44.860
different way. It has got to be much more
data-based. Do we have the analytical capability

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:51.100
and will we generate the right insight in
the right time? What about culture, behaviours,

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:57.350
collaboration, partnerships? We are not always
good at that either. The 'how' as well as

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:58.250
the 'what'.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:05.250
Karen Wheeler: Absolutely. That is fundamental.
One of those pieces of work under the key

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:10.819
enablers is the work that we are doing within
our own organisation to improve our own capabilities.

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:16.519
There are definitely some capabilities that
we do need to improve in terms of data and

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:23.470
information so that we can use the right strategic
insight to create the best strategies for

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:30.470
the future. Not only the capabilities, as
you say, it is critical that we start to change

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:39.350
the way that we operate inside our organisation
and with our partners. Working really closely

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:46.350
with our partners as a routine is fundamental.
Working closely with patient groups and Citizen

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:54.050
Groups so that we properly understand what
they want to achieve out of all of the change

00:04:54.150 --> 00:05:01.150
that we are doing. That is another cultural
change for us to continue to make that journey.

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:08.919
And, perhaps most importantly, working in
a way which facilitates and enables local

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:16.710
change with less focus on performance management
associated with targets - even though performance

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:24.070
management is absolutely critical to ensure
that we continue to deliver our service targets

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:24.650
as well.

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:29.000
Ciaran Devane: Are you confident we are putting
enough weight behind it? If we look at NHS

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.740
Citizen yesterday, there was one quote which
was fantastic which was, 'This feels like

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:39.500
real consultation, not like so often in the
past.' You can either see that as brilliantly

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:45.020
encouraging or you can say are we confident
that as we go out and about, trying to make

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:50.100
the change happen, we will truly engage individuals,
patient organisations, clinicians, partners?

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:56.510
That is a skill. It is not just something
you say. Are we going to be able to develop

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:57.600
that and deliver that?

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:04.449
Karen Wheeler: It is absolutely something
that we are working on. Tim has set up, with

00:06:04.449 --> 00:06:11.449
Victor chairing a Task &amp; Finish Group, specifically
to ensure that across the organisation we

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:23.770
are, in all of our business areas, working
differently in order to do that. I, like you,

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:32.510
do not underestimate the scale of the challenge
in people being able to make that change.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:37.540
That said, there are lots of good areas where
we are already doing that. We need to work

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:42.100
on the good practice that already exists.
We know, for example, there are many CCGs

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:46.380
as well who do that as a matter of course.
We need to learn from their good practice

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.199
as well as build it into our own processes.

00:06:49.199 --> 00:06:50.500
Malcolm Grant: David?

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:58.880
David Roberts: Just picking up on Ciaran's
theme, it is good to see the alignment between

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:05.419
the financial dynamic and the Business Plan
objectives. I guess the other key side to

00:07:05.419 --> 00:07:12.979
this is have we got the appropriate people,
leadership, talent to be able to focus on

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:20.050
what are critical priorities for patients?
Are we sure that we can mobilise them quickly

00:07:21.449 --> 00:07:22.139
enough?

00:07:22.139 --> 00:07:29.139
Karen Wheeler: There is a piece of work that
we have been doing, which is again something

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:37.350
that we will be discussing later on today,
about leadership improvement. Ed has been

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:44.669
chairing a piece of work around that, which
is precisely to look at how we can ensure

00:07:44.669 --> 00:07:51.669
that the capability that we have in the system,
and particularly that we nationally fund and

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:58.680
support through NHS England, is directed and
aligned with the work that we need to do for

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:06.900
the Five Year Forward View and trying to connect
that more explicitly with the local systems

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:13.949
that are responsible for making those changes
happen but connect it very clearly with the

00:08:13.949 --> 00:08:17.949
strategic direction of the Five Year Forward
View. Yes, that is a piece of the work. That

00:08:17.949 --> 00:08:24.949
is one of our themes and priorities that we
will need to take forward this year.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:29.130
Malcolm Grant: Moira?

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:34.800
Moira Gibbs: The word 'commissioning' actually
appears on this slide only once. The role

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:36.079
that NHS England is playing in these is often
a leadership one, rather than a straightforward

00:08:36.079 --> 00:08:43.079
commissioning one. It is reliant on lots of
partners. How much consultation and how confident

00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:47.829
are you that the partners are aligned with
the priorities here?

00:08:47.829 --> 00:08:54.829
Karen Wheeler: We have obviously talked to
our partners about our emerging Business Plan

00:08:55.730 --> 00:09:02.679
and we have seen their emerging business plans
as well. Clearly, we each as organisations

00:09:02.679 --> 00:09:09.350
have a different focus on the work that needs
to be done, but the core of this is that what

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:14.129
we are building our Business Plan around is
what we see as strategically essential to

00:09:14.129 --> 00:09:18.509
support the Five Year Forward View. Each of
those organisations are doing exactly the

00:09:18.509 --> 00:09:25.509
same. I am very confident that, not only collectively,
but also individually around the individual

00:09:26.069 --> 00:09:32.529
priorities, we are working extremely closely.
Undoubtedly though this is something that

00:09:32.529 --> 00:09:39.459
we need to spend even more time on and make
just the normal way of doing our business,

00:09:39.459 --> 00:09:44.999
in ensuring that everything we do, we do collectively.
I think we are putting a lot of energy into

00:09:44.999 --> 00:09:47.819
trying to do that now.

00:09:47.819 --> 00:09:49.509
Malcolm Grant: Margaret?

00:09:49.509 --> 00:09:56.170
Margaret Casely-Hayford: The foundations for
change include an enormous emphasis on digital

00:09:56.170 --> 00:10:07.630
improvement and my question is can we assume
that the digital agenda includes a real focus

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:15.860
on understanding and preparing for the best
that we can achieve in terms of security?

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:29.180
I suppose this is a question that sits between
you and Tim, but that requires a massive oversight

00:10:29.290 --> 00:10:32.009
capability from our perspective.

00:10:32.009 --> 00:10:41.109
Karen Wheeler: I will perhaps let Tim answer
that one. As far as NHS England is concerned:

00:10:42.239 --> 00:10:50.619
absolutely while we have not particularly
majored on security in our priorities, it

00:10:50.869 --> 00:10:57.869
is core to our business-as-usual. It is absolutely
fundamental to everything that we do and we

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:05.050
are continually improving everything that
we are doing around data security and particularly

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:10.529
cyber security, given the risk that we all
know is there. Tim, perhaps you want to talk

00:11:10.529 --> 00:11:11.639
about the NHS priority?

00:11:11.639 --> 00:11:15.689
Tim Kelsey: As we will discuss a little bit
later on, in one aspect we are wanting to

00:11:15.689 --> 00:11:19.649
very urgently make the additional offer, not
just to our clinical colleagues, but also

00:11:19.649 --> 00:11:25.379
to patients and citizens. The quid quo pro
is that we can demonstrate that we have set

00:11:25.379 --> 00:11:31.959
a new standard in the way in which we will
do our level best to be at the cutting edge

00:11:31.959 --> 00:11:38.959
of science in delivering secure data sharing
environments. There are lots of real challenges

00:11:39.499 --> 00:11:45.329
there, many of which have never been developed
anywhere. How do you handle identity assurance

00:11:45.329 --> 00:11:51.639
at a scale? In this kind of context that has
never really been developed before. We have

00:11:51.639 --> 00:11:55.079
got lots of questions we have not answered
but just to reassure you this is a fundamentally

00:11:55.079 --> 00:12:00.139
important part of delivering the Five Year
Forward View. In my view without the underlying

00:12:00.139 --> 00:12:05.579
digital architecture existing we cannot deliver
the kind of integration, the communal opportunities

00:12:05.579 --> 00:12:06.629
that we want to provide.

00:12:06.629 --> 00:12:09.429
Malcolm Grant: Thank you. Noel?

00:12:09.429 --> 00:12:16.429
Noel Gordon: Karen, in one sense this Business
Plan is of course now designed primarily as

00:12:16.879 --> 00:12:23.089
a means of communication with our people within
the NHS. In subsequent incarnations of this

00:12:23.089 --> 00:12:28.980
it also becomes a series of pledges that we
are going to make to patients, public and

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:35.980
citizenship more generally. Will we eventually
in subsequent incarnations become more specific

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:39.269
about what outcomes we are going to deliver?

00:12:39.269 --> 00:12:45.079
Karen Wheeler: Yes. I think that is really
important. Clearly some of the work that we

00:12:45.079 --> 00:12:52.079
have outlined in the Business Plan is very
clear and well-established and some is absolutely

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:57.299
new and fresh-thinking based on the Five Year
Forward View. However, in both of those areas

00:12:57.299 --> 00:13:03.239
we need to connect it with the outcomes that
we are trying to achieve and deliver for patients.

00:13:03.239 --> 00:13:10.239
That is always tricky in that we want to make
it very real and visible for patients and

00:13:12.149 --> 00:13:18.499
for staff as to what will be the difference.
We want to do that in a way which sets a stretching

00:13:18.499 --> 00:13:25.499
agenda and targets. Equally we do not want
to get into more target and performance management,

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:35.739
I guess, around the Business Plan, but be
clear where we are trying to get to. Yes,

00:13:35.739 --> 00:13:40.579
absolutely, we will be aiming to try to link
this to target outcomes.

00:13:40.579 --> 00:13:42.559
Noel Gordon: Thank you.

00:13:42.559 --> 00:13:48.489
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Karen. I think that
the Board can reflect on, first of all, the

00:13:48.489 --> 00:13:55.489
fact from last year's Business Plan with 31
priorities we have come down to ten. Secondly,

00:13:56.129 --> 00:14:03.069
the Business Plan that we will sign off I
think is the product of quite a lot of hard

00:14:03.069 --> 00:14:08.259
work and development which involves the Non-Executive
Directors as well as the Executive Directors.

00:14:08.259 --> 00:14:15.259
There is not a culture clash. There is a culture
blend from those with extensive experience

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:20.230
in the NHS and those who have extensive experience
in other environments as to what a business

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:21.220
plan might look like.

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:27.399
I hope that we have captured the best of both
worlds but certainly, as a Board, our emphasis

00:14:27.399 --> 00:14:34.399
I think is on simplicity, clarity, an ability
to communicate to those whom we employ and

00:14:35.489 --> 00:14:40.239
across the NHS more generally, what it is
we regard as absolutely top of the list, an

00:14:40.239 --> 00:14:45.139
ability to assure the Department of Health
that we are in a position to satisfy the objectives

00:14:45.139 --> 00:14:50.999
of the mandate and an ability to ensure the
Board at large that we know what is happening,

00:14:50.999 --> 00:14:55.629
what we are committed to, how we measure it
and how we hold the Executive to account.

00:14:55.629 --> 00:15:02.629
I think that the Business Plan is on track.
I do not think these things are ever the perfect

00:15:02.759 --> 00:15:07.139
finished article. However, it has been a very
valuable experience for us all.

00:15:07.139 --> 00:15:13.739
On that note may I invite the Board to approve,
in principle, the Business Plan and to authorise

00:15:13.739 --> 00:15:20.449
the Chief Executive and me to do the final
editing and to sign off the fully polished

00:15:20.449 --> 00:15:24.819
version? I think I have the assent of the
Board. Thank you very much.

